 AI Website Auditor - Complete Windows 11 Installation Guide
Version: 1.0
Purpose: Install the AI Website Auditor on a Windows 11 laptop using WSL2 (Ubuntu).
Total Time: ~30-45 minutes

📋 Prerequisites
Windows 11 laptop

Internet connection

Administrator rights on the laptop

The backup file: ai_website_auditor_backup.zip (from Google Drive or USB)

🔧 PART 1: Enable WSL2 on Windows 11
Step 1.1: Press the Windows Key on your keyboard.

Step 1.2: Type PowerShell.

Step 1.3: Right-click on Windows PowerShell and select "Run as administrator".

Step 1.4: In the blue PowerShell window, type this command and press Enter:

powershell
wsl --install
Step 1.5: Wait for the installation to complete (5-10 minutes). It will automatically install Ubuntu.

Step 1.6: Restart your computer when prompted.

🐧 PART 2: Launch Ubuntu and Set Up
Step 2.1: Press the Windows Key and type Ubuntu.

Step 2.2: Click on the Ubuntu app to launch it.

Step 2.3: Wait for the installation to finish. When prompted:

Enter new UNIX username: Type user (or your preferred name) and press Enter.

Enter new UNIX password: Type a password (you won't see it as you type) and press Enter.

Retype new UNIX password: Type it again and press Enter.

⚠️ Remember this password! You'll need it for sudo commands.

Step 2.4: Update Ubuntu by running:

bash
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
Type your password when prompted and press Enter. Wait for it to complete.

🛠️ PART 3: Install Required Tools
Step 3.1: Install Python, Git, and other essential tools:

bash
sudo apt install python3 python3-pip python3-venv git curl wget golang-go nmap -y
Step 3.2: Set up Go (required for subdomain discovery):

bash
export PATH=$PATH:~/go/bin
echo 'export PATH=$PATH:~/go/bin' >> ~/.bashrc
source ~/.bashrc
Step 3.3: Install Subfinder (subdomain discovery tool):

bash
go install -v github.com/projectdiscovery/subfinder/v2/cmd/subfinder@latest
Step 3.4: Install Waybackurls (URL discovery tool):

bash
go install github.com/tomnomnom/waybackurls@latest
📁 PART 4: Copy Files from Backup
Step 4.1: In Windows, locate your backup file ai_website_auditor_backup.zip. (Download it from Google Drive or copy from USB.)

Step 4.2: Extract the zip file. You should see a folder called ai_website_auditor_backup.

Step 4.3: In the Ubuntu terminal, copy the files from Windows to Ubuntu:

bash
cp /mnt/c/Users/YourUsername/Downloads/ai_website_auditor_backup/* ~/
⚠️ Important: Replace YourUsername with your actual Windows username.
(For example: /mnt/c/Users/rupendra/Downloads/...)

Step 4.4: Rename the folder and navigate into it:

bash
cd ~
mv ai_website_auditor_backup ai-website-auditor
cd ai-website-auditor
🐍 PART 5: Set Up Python Environment
Step 5.1: Create a virtual environment:

bash
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
Step 5.2: Install all required Python packages:

bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
Step 5.3: Make the launcher script executable:

bash
chmod +x run.sh
🚀 PART 6: Run the App
Step 6.1: Start the app:

bash
./run.sh
Step 6.2: Open your browser and go to:
👉 http://localhost:8501

🔧 PART 7: Fix Nmap (Optional)
If you get a "Nmap requires root privileges" error, run the app with sudo:

bash
sudo -E ./run.sh
(The -E flag keeps your environment variables, including the PATH to subfinder.)

🔍 PART 8: Test the App
Enter a website URL (e.g., https://mysticsoundhealer.com)

Click "Start Audit"

Wait for the audit to complete

Check the Bug Bounty Reconnaissance section for subdomains, URLs, and open ports

📁 PART 9: Find Your Reports
All reports are automatically saved in the reports/ folder:

bash
ls -la ~/ai-website-auditor/reports/
You can open them in your browser:

bash
firefox ~/ai-website-auditor/reports/technical_report_*.html
❓ Troubleshooting
Issue	Solution
subfinder: command not found	Run export PATH=$PATH:~/go/bin then try again
Nmap permission error	Run with sudo -E ./run.sh
App won't start	Run cd ~/ai-website-auditor && source venv/bin/activate && ./run.sh
Port 8501 already in use	Kill the process: sudo lsof -i :8501 then sudo kill -9 PID
ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'distutils'	This is a harmless browser-opening error. Ignore it.
